WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.640 --> 00:00:04.100
Whenever any movie is released, there are
bound to be some people that like it, and

00:00:04.100 --> 00:00:05.400
others that don't.

00:00:05.400 --> 00:00:09.230
After all, beauty is in the eye of the beholder,
and art is almost always subjective.

00:00:09.230 --> 00:00:13.740
A film's popularity is usually just a matter
of taste and timing, but every once in a while,

00:00:13.740 --> 00:00:16.430
a film's very premise can turn people off
at the outset.

00:00:16.430 --> 00:00:20.440
In the case of these movies, the stories had
some people so enraged that they wished the

00:00:20.440 --> 00:00:23.409
movies had never even been made.

00:00:23.409 --> 00:00:24.659
Caligula

00:00:24.659 --> 00:00:28.660
Some films are created by great artists who
are inspired by a grand vision for a work

00:00:28.660 --> 00:00:31.830
of art that can inspire humanity to higher
ideals.

00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:35.910
And then there's Caligula, the brainchild
of Penthouse's Bob Guccione, who essentially

00:00:35.910 --> 00:00:39.609
convinced the world that the picture was going
to be high art, only to pull a bait and switch

00:00:39.609 --> 00:00:41.789
and turn it into an all-out sexcapade.

00:00:41.789 --> 00:00:46.011
Given his history as an adult magazine magnate,
it's probably not surprising that the film

00:00:46.011 --> 00:00:50.460
has some risque [risk-AY] action, but what
was surprising is how much skin is shown throughout

00:00:50.460 --> 00:00:51.460
the film.

00:00:51.460 --> 00:00:55.870
"It has an irresistible mixture of art and
genitals in it."

00:00:55.870 --> 00:00:59.730
The fact that it stars great actors like Malcolm
McDowell and Helen Mirren, and was written

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:03.649
by Gore Vidal is also something of a shock
when you see it because while the stars and

00:01:03.649 --> 00:01:07.860
writer thought they were making the next great
historical epic, Guccione secretly shot a

00:01:07.860 --> 00:01:10.810
bunch of hardcore scenes to splice into the
film during the edit.

00:01:10.810 --> 00:01:14.860
The result was so shocking that the rest of
the cast and crew revolted, and the film was

00:01:14.860 --> 00:01:15.860
banned.

00:01:15.860 --> 00:01:19.549
Vidal had his name removed from the film altogether,
and the actors were allegedly furious.

00:01:19.549 --> 00:01:21.060
"It's such a mish-mash.

00:01:21.060 --> 00:01:25.070
There's some wonderful stuff in it, and some
absolute garbage."

00:01:25.070 --> 00:01:27.320
The Last Temptation of Christ

00:01:27.320 --> 00:01:32.310
As an adaptation of the 1955 book of the same
name, Martin Scorsese's The Last Temptation

00:01:32.310 --> 00:01:35.930
of Christ already had a legacy of controversy
before it was even filmed.

00:01:35.930 --> 00:01:41.390
The novel, from Greek author Nikos Kazantzakis,
infuriated the Greek Orthodox Church and inspired

00:01:41.390 --> 00:01:43.799
religious protests upon its publication.

00:01:43.799 --> 00:01:47.689
So it probably wasn't much of a surprise when
the same groups rose up against the production

00:01:47.689 --> 00:01:49.100
of Scorsese's film.

00:01:49.100 --> 00:01:53.270
What was surprising, however, was how broad
the resistance had become, as some Catholic

00:01:53.270 --> 00:01:57.579
and evangelical Protestant communities joined
with the original Greek Orthodox Church in

00:01:57.579 --> 00:01:59.119
their condemnation of the film.

00:01:59.119 --> 00:02:01.789
So, why did Last Temptation draw so much pushback?

00:02:01.789 --> 00:02:05.590
The film portrays Christ as a human figure
wrestling with his own divinity, wracked with

00:02:05.590 --> 00:02:08.970
various temptations like lust, fear, doubt,
and anger.

00:02:08.970 --> 00:02:13.480
The titular "last" temptation involves an
extended sequence during which Jesus renounces

00:02:13.480 --> 00:02:15.870
his divinity, marries, and has children.

00:02:15.870 --> 00:02:20.290
Though Jesus later rejects the possibility
and sacrifices himself, the very fact that

00:02:20.290 --> 00:02:25.430
the religious icon was presented in such human
light was rejected by the church masses.

00:02:25.430 --> 00:02:26.980
United 93

00:02:26.980 --> 00:02:31.150
Coming off the success of The Bourne Supremacy,
director Paul Greengrass chose to turn his

00:02:31.150 --> 00:02:35.150
attention to a real-world tragedy, the events
of September 11th, 2001.

00:02:35.150 --> 00:02:40.530
His film, United 93, went into production
in 2005, just a few short years after the

00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:43.030
largest terrorist attack in U.S. history.

00:02:43.030 --> 00:02:47.210
With America still involved in two wars wrapped
up in the resulting War on Terror, the film

00:02:47.210 --> 00:02:49.950
was primed to set off strong reactions — and
it did.

00:02:49.950 --> 00:02:54.590
When the trailer for the film was first shown
in theaters, it sparked outcries of protest.

00:02:54.590 --> 00:02:58.209
People accused Hollywood of callously cashing
in on the horror of that day, without any

00:02:58.209 --> 00:03:01.250
regard or respect for the people directly
affected by it.

00:03:01.250 --> 00:03:05.630
The backlash was pronounced enough that distributor
Universal had to publicly announce that they

00:03:05.630 --> 00:03:09.340
would not be pulling the trailer from movie
theaters, although the studio did agree to

00:03:09.340 --> 00:03:13.410
donate ten percent of the opening weekend
gross to a 9/11-related charity.

00:03:13.410 --> 00:03:17.310
Fortunately in this case, the finished film
allayed concerns of a crass capitalization

00:03:17.310 --> 00:03:18.310
of tragedy.

00:03:18.310 --> 00:03:22.690
Reviews were glowing, and family members of
victims found United 93 to be respectful to

00:03:22.690 --> 00:03:24.340
the memory of those lost.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:28.310
But it also became an example of a film with
genuine artistic merit that had to weather

00:03:28.310 --> 00:03:32.780
initial doubts in order to at last be embraced
as a triumph.

00:03:32.780 --> 00:03:33.980
Watchmen

00:03:33.980 --> 00:03:36.819
Once upon a time, comic book superheroes were
for kids.

00:03:36.819 --> 00:03:40.800
They wore brightly colored costumes, fought
bad guys, and always did the right thing.

00:03:40.800 --> 00:03:45.190
By the mid-'80s, though, a number of comics
presented a darker, bloodier, more adult look

00:03:45.190 --> 00:03:48.200
at heroes and their worlds, and one such was
Watchmen.

00:03:48.200 --> 00:03:52.330
The series was written by Alan Moore and arrived
as a brilliant deconstruction of the entire

00:03:52.330 --> 00:03:56.750
superhero genre that showed the flawed humanity
underneath the spandex.

00:03:56.750 --> 00:04:01.269
Watchmen was a project he'd created for industry
titan DC Comics, which was itself owned by

00:04:01.269 --> 00:04:06.240
film studio Warner Bros., which meant that,
at any time, WB could take his creation and

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:07.769
turn it into a movie.

00:04:07.769 --> 00:04:12.400
After the success of the comic adaptation
300, WB gave the rights to Watchmen to director

00:04:12.400 --> 00:04:15.010
Zack Snyder — and Moore was furious.

00:04:15.010 --> 00:04:19.860
Calling the very idea of a Watchmen movie
"regurgitated worms," Moore insisted that

00:04:19.860 --> 00:04:23.820
his work was intended exclusively for the
comic medium and was never intended to be

00:04:23.820 --> 00:04:25.780
adapted into anything else.

00:04:25.780 --> 00:04:30.390
"It might entertain people for a few minutes,
but I'm not interested in it because it's

00:04:30.390 --> 00:04:32.360
not the story that I was trying to tell."

00:04:32.360 --> 00:04:36.490
But his protests fell on deaf ears, and Watchmen
arrived in theaters in 2009.

00:04:36.490 --> 00:04:40.780
When it did, Moore's name was nowhere to be
found: he had himself removed from the credits

00:04:40.780 --> 00:04:44.260
and even rejected any share of the film's
profits.

00:04:44.260 --> 00:04:45.640
The Interview

00:04:45.640 --> 00:04:49.600
Seth Rogen and James Franco have made quite
a few successful films together, including

00:04:49.600 --> 00:04:52.900
Pineapple Express, This Is the End, and The
Disaster Artist.

00:04:52.900 --> 00:04:57.610
But their 2014 political comedy The Interview
was met with the kind of pre-release backlash

00:04:57.610 --> 00:04:59.900
apparently no one in Hollywood expected.

00:04:59.900 --> 00:05:03.960
The screwball comedy featured the two as a
pair of idiotic talk show producers contracted

00:05:03.960 --> 00:05:06.300
by the CIA to take out Kim Jong-Un.

00:05:06.300 --> 00:05:09.600
It's not the first time a North Korean dictator
got spoofed on-screen, of course.

00:05:09.600 --> 00:05:12.760
"Why aren't more people intelligent like me?"

00:05:12.760 --> 00:05:15.630
But this time, Kim fought back against the
film's release.

00:05:15.630 --> 00:05:20.000
The isolated nation doesn't receive a lot
of Hollywood exports, so its so-called "supreme

00:05:20.000 --> 00:05:21.850
leader" might have seemed like a safe target.

00:05:21.850 --> 00:05:26.190
However, Sony underestimated just how much
offense the dictator would take at the film's

00:05:26.190 --> 00:05:27.190
release.

00:05:27.190 --> 00:05:30.880
He reportedly ordered a cyberattack on Sony
Pictures, which led to the leak of a trove

00:05:30.880 --> 00:05:32.390
of embarrassing emails.

00:05:32.390 --> 00:05:36.591
While the email leak was bad enough, the hackers
later threatened violent action if the film

00:05:36.591 --> 00:05:37.591
was ever released.

00:05:37.591 --> 00:05:42.270
Facing a PR disaster, Sony pulled the film
from theaters, a decision criticized by President

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:43.270
Obama.

00:05:43.270 --> 00:05:45.300
"I wish they had spoken to me first."

00:05:45.300 --> 00:05:49.070
However, in defiance of the dictator, The
Interview was almost immediately made available

00:05:49.070 --> 00:05:53.540
on streaming services, so it was still entirely
possible for audiences to watch Franco and

00:05:53.540 --> 00:05:57.090
Rogen carry out their plot against a fictional
Kim.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:58.090
Slender Man

00:05:58.090 --> 00:06:02.330
Most of the time, internet memes are meant
to be good-natured distractions from the malaise

00:06:02.330 --> 00:06:07.770
of modern life, but when the Slender Man trend
came along in 2009, it was anything but fanciful

00:06:07.770 --> 00:06:08.770
fun.

00:06:08.770 --> 00:06:12.900
As the viral myth grew from one creepy picture
to the next, the character inspired some disturbed

00:06:12.900 --> 00:06:15.210
teenagers to commit terrible crimes in real-life.

00:06:15.210 --> 00:06:20.300
So, when Sony Screen Gems decided to turn
the internet fad into a film, a lot of people

00:06:20.300 --> 00:06:21.300
were not happy.

00:06:21.300 --> 00:06:25.560
The father of one of the attackers, for example,
protested the film for being a sensationalist

00:06:25.560 --> 00:06:29.350
cash grab, and a petition was even started
to halt the film's release.

00:06:29.350 --> 00:06:33.930
Despite all that, though,the film is currently
still slated for a fall 2018 debut, at which

00:06:33.930 --> 00:06:36.660
point audiences can judge its merits for themselves.

00:06:36.660 --> 00:06:37.660
Thanks for watching!

00:06:37.660 --> 00:06:39.970
